Title: verify-gate: comment-only edits never block on authorisation scope
Created: 2026-10-20
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-10-20T05:34Z Minh Ha-Duong created

--- body ---
## Context
sen-causal-reading PR #25 (ticket 0026), 2026-10-20: `/verify-gate` round 2
returned ESCALATE partly because a fix agent deleted one stray word ("les")
from a LaTeX comment in `rapport/equivalence.tex`. The gate classed it as an
unauthorised `rapport/` prose edit ("FX1-scope ... blocking until
authorised"). The comment never reaches the PDF. The raid brief had said "do
not edit rapport/"; the main session's later message allowing reference and
comment fixes did not count as author authorisation. The author called the
block absurd. The brief side is fixed in project memory; this ticket is the
gate side.

Under the severity floor (rules/workflow.md: when a guard misfires, prefer
deleting or narrowing it over growing it), the fix narrows the guard.

## Decision (author, 2026-10-20)
A change confined to comments (LaTeX `%`, code comments, Markdown/HTML
comments) is never a scope or authorisation blocker. At most it is recorded
as `nofollow:`.

## Design notes
- Scope containment and adherence-scope checks: when a hunk touches only
  comment lines in a protected prose path, record it, do not block.
- Also consider: an exception the caller grants during a run should be
  recognised when stated in the PR body, so it need not reach the author.
- Keep the guard for typeset text and argument changes.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] verify-gate (and any adherence scope rule it relies on) treats comment-only hunks in protected paths as non-blocking
- [ ] A test or worked example: a `%`-comment edit under a protected path yields no blocker; a typeset-sentence edit still does
- [ ] `make check` passes
